Fortuna</em> are LED light sculptures created by Grimanesa Amoros.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Noting that <em>Uros Island</em>&rsquo;s smooth LED domes contrast with <em>Fortuna</em>&rsquo;s tangled LED tubes, the sentence emphasizes a difference between the two sculptures rather than a similarity. Choice B is incorrect. Contrasting the respective sizes of <em>Uros Island</em> and <em>Fortuna</em>, the sentence emphasizes a difference between the two sculptures rather than a similarity. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence describes <em>Uros Island</em>, it doesn&rsquo;t mention <em>Fortuna</em> or emphasize any similarity between the two sculptures.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061192,"updateDate":1755117061192}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061192, 1755117061192),
    ($SATQ$72ae9bca$SATQ$, $SATQ$fc10c5d1-890e-4de3-b3ca-9260ae5befb3$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>In the early 1900s, suffragists organized marches for women&rsquo;s voting rights.</li>
<li>Suffragists in the United Kingdom marched from Edinburgh to London.</li>
<li>This march began on October 12, 1912, and ended on November 16, 1912.</li>
<li>Suffragists in the United States marched from New York City to Albany, New York.</li>
<li>This march began on December 16, 1912, and ended on December 28, 1912.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to emphasize the order in which the two marches occurred. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"79ab9184-d108-4b92-810c-63a201f81aab","content":"<p>After suffragists in the UK marched from Edinburgh to London, suffragists in the US marched from New York City to Albany, New York.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"7fd5de75-0f6d-4f9d-92d2-33758d9ad3da","content":"<p>In the early 1900s, suffragists in the UK and the US marched for women&rsquo;s voting rights.</p>"},{"id":"4e0df8ed-075f-4e65-9f07-2eaf885c46d4","content":"<p>A march from New York City to Albany, New York, was followed by one that began in Edinburgh and ended in London. &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"541dc5ad-9706-41bc-bd93-28303b198dcb","content":"<p>From October 12 to November 16, 1912, suffragists in the UK marched from Edinburgh to London.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["79ab9184-d108-4b92-810c-63a201f81aab"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The sentence emphasizes the order in which the two marches occurred, correctly indicating that the US march (December 16 to December 28, 1912) occurred after the march in the UK (October 12 to November 16, 1912).</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. While the sentence mentions that both marches took place in the early 1900s, it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the order in which the two marches occurred. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence does emphasize the order in which the two marches occurred, the order is incorrect. The UK march took place from October 12 to November 16, 1912, which was before the US march (December 1912). Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence specifies the dates of the UK march, it doesn&rsquo;t mention the US march or emphasize the order in which the two marches occurred.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"72ae9bca","external_id":"fc10c5d1-890e-4de3-b3ca-9260ae5befb3","vaultid":null,"uId":"917754e4-466e-410b-9e02-70b085258b75","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In the early 1900s, suffragists organized marches for women&rsquo;s voting rights.</li>\n<li>Suffragists in the United Kingdom marched from Edinburgh to London.</li>\n<li>This march began on October 12, 1912, and ended on November 16, 1912.</li>\n<li>Suffragists in the United States marched from New York City to Albany, New York.</li>\n<li>This march began on December 16, 1912, and ended on December 28, 1912.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to emphasize the order in which the two marches occurred. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"79ab9184-d108-4b92-810c-63a201f81aab","content":"<p>After suffragists in the UK marched from Edinburgh to London, suffragists in the US marched from New York City to Albany, New York.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"7fd5de75-0f6d-4f9d-92d2-33758d9ad3da","content":"<p>In the early 1900s, suffragists in the UK and the US marched for women&rsquo;s voting rights.</p>"},{"id":"4e0df8ed-075f-4e65-9f07-2eaf885c46d4","content":"<p>A march from New York City to Albany, New York, was followed by one that began in Edinburgh and ended in London. &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"541dc5ad-9706-41bc-bd93-28303b198dcb","content":"<p>From October 12 to November 16, 1912, suffragists in the UK marched from Edinburgh to London.</p>"}],"keys":["79ab9184-d108-4b92-810c-63a201f81aab"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The sentence emphasizes the order in which the two marches occurred, correctly indicating that the US march (December 16 to December 28, 1912) occurred after the march in the UK (October 12 to November 16, 1912).</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. While the sentence mentions that both marches took place in the early 1900s, it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the order in which the two marches occurred. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence does emphasize the order in which the two marches occurred, the order is incorrect. The UK march took place from October 12 to November 16, 1912, which was before the US march (December 1912). Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence specifies the dates of the UK march, it doesn&rsquo;t mention the US march or emphasize the order in which the two marches occurred.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323034,"updateDate":1730147323034}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323034, 1730147323034),
    ($SATQ$74028acf$SATQ$, $SATQ$dfe91e9e-0f76-4130-80c1-8a8a439b700d$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Topographic maps show the elevation of landforms above sea level. Bathymetric maps, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> show the elevation of landforms below the sea, providing valuable information to marine geophysicists like Claudia Flores, who studies seismic data from the northeastern Caribbean.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"bf0ecdac-5109-4fe4-b4d2-6daaa80b0f7a","content":"<p>in conclusion,</p>"},{"id":"feeb3438-a965-45a9-8e0b-a4d2f6727b08","content":"<p>for example,</p>"},{"id":"daa06778-5ebe-43d7-9ecc-fffa9a5f8a98","content":"<p>by contrast,</p>"},{"id":"ac2d1f00-e0b8-4c12-9864-61b0f3c6a688","content":"<p>afterward,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["daa06778-5ebe-43d7-9ecc-fffa9a5f8a98"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. &ldquo;By contrast&rdquo; logically signals that the information about bathymetric maps in this sentence&mdash;that they show landforms&rsquo; elevation below the sea&mdash;presents a direct contrast to the previous information about topographic maps showing landforms&rsquo; elevation above sea level.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;in conclusion&rdquo; illogically signals that the information about bathymetric maps in this sentence concludes or summarizes the previous information about topographic maps. Instead, the sentence provides contrasting information about bathymetric maps. Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;for example&rdquo; illogically signals that the information about bathymetric maps in this sentence exemplifies the previous information about topographic maps. Instead, the sentence provides contrasting information about bathymetric maps. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;afterward&rdquo; illogically signals that this sentence describes an event that chronologically follows the previous information about topographic maps. Instead, the sentence provides contrasting information about bathymetric maps.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"74028acf","external_id":"dfe91e9e-0f76-4130-80c1-8a8a439b700d","vaultid":null,"uId":"6fa770a6-a462-48ac-ae4e-5ecbdec58e7c","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Topographic maps show the elevation of landforms above sea level. Bathymetric maps, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> show the elevation of landforms below the sea, providing valuable information to marine geophysicists like Claudia Flores, who studies seismic data from the northeastern Caribbean.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"bf0ecdac-5109-4fe4-b4d2-6daaa80b0f7a","content":"<p>in conclusion,</p>"},{"id":"feeb3438-a965-45a9-8e0b-a4d2f6727b08","content":"<p>for example,</p>"},{"id":"daa06778-5ebe-43d7-9ecc-fffa9a5f8a98","content":"<p>by contrast,</p>"},{"id":"ac2d1f00-e0b8-4c12-9864-61b0f3c6a688","content":"<p>afterward,</p>"}],"keys":["daa06778-5ebe-43d7-9ecc-fffa9a5f8a98"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. &ldquo;By contrast&rdquo; logically signals that the information about bathymetric maps in this sentence&mdash;that they show landforms&rsquo; elevation below the sea&mdash;presents a direct contrast to the previous information about topographic maps showing landforms&rsquo; elevation above sea level.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;in conclusion&rdquo; illogically signals that the information about bathymetric maps in this sentence concludes or summarizes the previous information about topographic maps. Instead, the sentence provides contrasting information about bathymetric maps. Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;for example&rdquo; illogically signals that the information about bathymetric maps in this sentence exemplifies the previous information about topographic maps. Instead, the sentence provides contrasting information about bathymetric maps. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;afterward&rdquo; illogically signals that this sentence describes an event that chronologically follows the previous information about topographic maps. Instead, the sentence provides contrasting information about bathymetric maps.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771170,"updateDate":1776362771170}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771170, 1776362771170),
    ($SATQ$74149724$SATQ$, $SATQ$e532b84b-ee31-43c3-88ad-148eedbd1abe$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$346f4b56-3c1c-4cea-a3c8-b0ed6ff8b0fa$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>John Carver was one of the 41 signatories of the Mayflower Compact.</li>
<li>The Mayflower Compact was a legal agreement among the pilgrims that immigrated to Plymouth Colony.</li>
<li>It was created in 1620 to establish a common government.</li>
<li>It states that the pilgrims who signed it wanted to &ldquo;plant the first colony in the northern parts of Virginia&rdquo; under King James.</li>
<li>Carver became the first governor of Plymouth Colony.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to specify the reason the Mayflower Compact was created. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"c2b6ab9c-631c-4420-8447-4841b07d8051","content":"<p>Stating that its signatories wanted to &ldquo;plant the first colony in the northern parts of Virginia,&rdquo; the Mayflower Compact was a legal agreement among the pilgrims that immigrated to Plymouth Colony.</p>"},{"id":"dc2af79f-d792-4010-b5ee-9884d2bea45c","content":"<p>Created in 1620, the Mayflower Compact states that the pilgrims wanted to &ldquo;plant the first colony in the northern parts of Virginia.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"1b587110-518e-41f6-a8e5-2212c3a3b83e","content":"<p>The Mayflower Compact was created to establish a common government among the pilgrims that immigrated to Plymouth Colony.</p>"},{"id":"66e56998-812f-4b98-90ea-bac50d4bf466","content":"<p>The Mayflower Compact had 41 signatories, including John Carver, the first governor of Plymouth Colony.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["1b587110-518e-41f6-a8e5-2212c3a3b83e"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The sentence specifies the reason the Mayflower Compact was created, noting that it was created to establish a common government among the pilgrims that immigrated to Plymouth Colony. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the sentence provides background information about the Mayflower Compact and notes the signatories&rsquo; goal for the colony, it doesn&rsquo;t specify why the compact was created. Choice B is incorrect. While the sentence provides background information about the Mayflower Compact and notes the signatories&rsquo; goal for the colony, it doesn&rsquo;t specify why the compact was created. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence specifies the number of pilgrims that signed the Mayflower Compact; it doesn&rsquo;t specify the reason the compact was created. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-024-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$4fc869d3-bec3-4ae8-9680-51acb0e28cbe$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"74149724","external_id":"e532b84b-ee31-43c3-88ad-148eedbd1abe","vaultid":"346f4b56-3c1c-4cea-a3c8-b0ed6ff8b0fa","uId":"37611ab8-80f1-4b0f-b0f4-a899372a1e58","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>John Carver was one of the 41 signatories of the Mayflower Compact.</li>\n<li>The Mayflower Compact was a legal agreement among the pilgrims that immigrated to Plymouth Colony.</li>\n<li>It was created in 1620 to establish a common government.</li>\n<li>It states that the pilgrims who signed it wanted to &ldquo;plant the first colony in the northern parts of Virginia&rdquo; under King James.</li>\n<li>Carver became the first governor of Plymouth Colony.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to specify the reason the Mayflower Compact was created. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"c2b6ab9c-631c-4420-8447-4841b07d8051","content":"<p>Stating that its signatories wanted to &ldquo;plant the first colony in the northern parts of Virginia,&rdquo; the Mayflower Compact was a legal agreement among the pilgrims that immigrated to Plymouth Colony.</p>"},{"id":"dc2af79f-d792-4010-b5ee-9884d2bea45c","content":"<p>Created in 1620, the Mayflower Compact states that the pilgrims wanted to &ldquo;plant the first colony in the northern parts of Virginia.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"1b587110-518e-41f6-a8e5-2212c3a3b83e","content":"<p>The Mayflower Compact was created to establish a common government among the pilgrims that immigrated to Plymouth Colony.</p>"},{"id":"66e56998-812f-4b98-90ea-bac50d4bf466","content":"<p>The Mayflower Compact had 41 signatories, including John Carver, the first governor of Plymouth Colony.</p>"}],"keys":["1b587110-518e-41f6-a8e5-2212c3a3b83e"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The sentence specifies the reason the Mayflower Compact was created, noting that it was created to establish a common government among the pilgrims that immigrated to Plymouth Colony. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the sentence provides background information about the Mayflower Compact and notes the signatories&rsquo; goal for the colony, it doesn&rsquo;t specify why the compact was created. Choice B is incorrect. While the sentence provides background information about the Mayflower Compact and notes the signatories&rsquo; goal for the colony, it doesn&rsquo;t specify why the compact was created. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence specifies the number of pilgrims that signed the Mayflower Compact; it doesn&rsquo;t specify the reason the compact was created. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-024-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"4fc869d3-bec3-4ae8-9680-51acb0e28cbe","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959318,"updateDate":1691007959318}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959318, 1691007959318),
    ($SATQ$7572131d$SATQ$, $SATQ$96de8f48-7567-43ab-8222-fa99e94d2ed8$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Elizabeth Catlett&rsquo;s sculpture <em>Recognition</em> (1970) shows two African American figures with rounded, indistinct features.</li>
<li>The figures reach out to each other in a pose that symbolizes a close, supportive relationship.</li>
<li>Her sculpture <em>Students Aspire</em> (1978) shows two African American figures with sharply defined features.</li>
<li>The figures hold an equal sign above their heads with one hand and embrace each other with the other hand.</li>
<li>This pose symbolizes their support for each other in the pursuit of equality.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the two sculptures. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"654b6bfa-910a-45eb-88c3-36e45df126e8","content":"<p>Catlett&rsquo;s <em>Students Aspire</em> depicts two figures supporting each other in the pursuit of equality.</p>"},{"id":"ca316b20-1528-4c6c-b427-59e76fb6d41e","content":"<p><em>Recognition</em> and <em>Students Aspire</em> both show African American figures in poses that symbolize supportive relationships.</p>"},{"id":"987da77b-be1d-400f-bbab-a98d2b41b7d1","content":"<p>Catlett completed <em>Recognition</em> in 1970 and <em>Students Aspire</em> in 1978.</p>"},{"id":"57ebe92c-77ae-4239-99f7-89ece4e94a86","content":"<p>The figures in <em>Recognition</em> have features that are rounded and indistinct, while the figures in <em>Students Aspire</em> have sharply defined features.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["ca316b20-1528-4c6c-b427-59e76fb6d41e"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The sentence emphasizes a similarity between the sculptures <em>Recognition</em> and <em>Students Aspire</em>, noting that both sculptures show African American figures in poses that symbolize supportive relationships.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence describes one of the sculptures; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize a similarity between the two sculptures. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence specifies the different years the sculptures were completed in; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize a similarity between the two sculptures. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes a difference between the two sculptures, noting that the figures in the sculptures have different feature definition; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize a similarity between the two sculptures.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"7572131d","external_id":"96de8f48-7567-43ab-8222-fa99e94d2ed8","vaultid":null,"uId":"6f9bef16-49a2-4e74-84b2-588b8b085209","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Elizabeth Catlett&rsquo;s sculpture <em>Recognition</em> (1970) shows two African American figures with rounded, indistinct features.</li>\n<li>The figures reach out to each other in a pose that symbolizes a close, supportive relationship.</li>\n<li>Her sculpture <em>Students Aspire</em> (1978) shows two African American figures with sharply defined features.</li>\n<li>The figures hold an equal sign above their heads with one hand and embrace each other with the other hand.</li>\n<li>This pose symbolizes their support for each other in the pursuit of equality.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the two sculptures. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"654b6bfa-910a-45eb-88c3-36e45df126e8","content":"<p>Catlett&rsquo;s <em>Students Aspire</em> depicts two figures supporting each other in the pursuit of equality.</p>"},{"id":"ca316b20-1528-4c6c-b427-59e76fb6d41e","content":"<p><em>Recognition</em> and <em>Students Aspire</em> both show African American figures in poses that symbolize supportive relationships.</p>"},{"id":"987da77b-be1d-400f-bbab-a98d2b41b7d1","content":"<p>Catlett completed <em>Recognition</em> in 1970 and <em>Students Aspire</em> in 1978.</p>"},{"id":"57ebe92c-77ae-4239-99f7-89ece4e94a86","content":"<p>The figures in <em>Recognition</em> have features that are rounded and indistinct, while the figures in <em>Students Aspire</em> have sharply defined features.</p>"}],"keys":["ca316b20-1528-4c6c-b427-59e76fb6d41e"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The sentence emphasizes a similarity between the sculptures <em>Recognition</em> and <em>Students Aspire</em>, noting that both sculptures show African American figures in poses that symbolize supportive relationships.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence describes one of the sculptures; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize a similarity between the two sculptures. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence specifies the different years the sculptures were completed in; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize a similarity between the two sculptures. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes a difference between the two sculptures, noting that the figures in the sculptures have different feature definition; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize a similarity between the two sculptures.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323048,"updateDate":1730147323048}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323048, 1730147323048),
    ($SATQ$77e3b3b3$SATQ$, $SATQ$b5f5a3c9-8e64-4839-ae5e-632c2bbaef48$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li><span data-ssml-say-as="date">2024</span>: Spain and Portugal sponsored a workshop to address recent encounters between Iberian orcas and marine vessels off the Iberian Peninsula.</li>
<li>Many of the 637 documented incidents involved pods of orcas damaging vessels by ramming, nudging, or biting the rudders.</li>
<li>Studies of Iberian orcas suggest recent increases in tuna populations have reduced the time the orcas spend hunting by 99%.</li>
<li>Researchers believe this shift has increased the number of interactions between marine vessels and understimulated orcas.</li>
<li>The workshop advised mariners to avoid orcas pending further testing of the efficacy of <span data-ssml-say-as="characters">TAST</span>, a harmless acoustic deterrent.</li>
</ul></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to make and support a claim about Iberian orca behavior. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"26f578a1-4bf2-474f-aed8-172a9d722276","content":"<p>The recent destructive behavior of Iberian orcas may be the result of understimulation, given that orcas&rsquo; interactions with marine vessels have increased as the orcas have spent less time hunting.</p>"},{"id":"9d03f825-506e-4cd1-90f2-66083b3aa13c","content":"<p>As supported by the workshop&rsquo;s analysis of 637 encounters between vessels and Iberian orcas, <span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">TAST</span> is a harmless and effective acoustic deterrent.</p>"},{"id":"07713f69-7d46-4ed4-b702-7c819bb7c0e8","content":"<p>In <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">2024</span>, Spain and Portugal sponsored a workshop that addressed incidents where pods of Iberian orcas were ramming, nudging, or biting the rudders of vessels.</p>"},{"id":"eb9a1ecb-5bf0-4a56-8cac-44bf6938a548","content":"<p>Tuna populations have increased by approximately 99% off the Iberian Peninsula due to changes in the hunting behavior of Iberian orcas.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["26f578a1-4bf2-474f-aed8-172a9d722276"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The sentence makes and supports a claim about Iberian orca behavior, claiming that the orcas&rsquo; destructive behavior may be the result of understimulation and citing as support the fact that as orcas have spent less time hunting (a stimulating activity), encounters with vessels have increased.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. While the sentence makes a claim about an orca deterrent, it misrepresents information from the notes. The workshop advised avoiding orcas pending further testing of TAST&rsquo;s efficacy; it didn&rsquo;t claim that TAST was effective. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence describes a workshop that addressed the orcas&rsquo; behavior; it doesn&rsquo;t make or support a claim about their behavior. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence does make a claim about orcas&rsquo; hunting behavior, it misrepresents information from the notes; the notes state that increased tuna populations have reduced orcas&rsquo; hunting time by 99%, not that tuna populations have increased by 99% because of the orcas&rsquo; behavior.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"77e3b3b3","external_id":"b5f5a3c9-8e64-4839-ae5e-632c2bbaef48","vaultid":null,"uId":"f7d43101-12f8-4aa8-8a8c-5534ba3118d8","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">2024</span>: Spain and Portugal sponsored a workshop to address recent encounters between Iberian orcas and marine vessels off the Iberian Peninsula.</li>\n<li>Many of the 637 documented incidents involved pods of orcas damaging vessels by ramming, nudging, or biting the rudders.</li>\n<li>Studies of Iberian orcas suggest recent increases in tuna populations have reduced the time the orcas spend hunting by 99%.</li>\n<li>Researchers believe this shift has increased the number of interactions between marine vessels and understimulated orcas.</li>\n<li>The workshop advised mariners to avoid orcas pending further testing of the efficacy of <span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">TAST</span>, a harmless acoustic deterrent.</li>\n</ul></p>","stem":"<p>The student wants to make and support a claim about Iberian orca behavior. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"26f578a1-4bf2-474f-aed8-172a9d722276","content":"<p>The recent destructive behavior of Iberian orcas may be the result of understimulation, given that orcas&rsquo; interactions with marine vessels have increased as the orcas have spent less time hunting.</p>"},{"id":"9d03f825-506e-4cd1-90f2-66083b3aa13c","content":"<p>As supported by the workshop&rsquo;s analysis of 637 encounters between vessels and Iberian orcas, <span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">TAST</span> is a harmless and effective acoustic deterrent.</p>"},{"id":"07713f69-7d46-4ed4-b702-7c819bb7c0e8","content":"<p>In <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">2024</span>, Spain and Portugal sponsored a workshop that addressed incidents where pods of Iberian orcas were ramming, nudging, or biting the rudders of vessels.</p>"},{"id":"eb9a1ecb-5bf0-4a56-8cac-44bf6938a548","content":"<p>Tuna populations have increased by approximately 99% off the Iberian Peninsula due to changes in the hunting behavior of Iberian orcas.</p>"}],"keys":["26f578a1-4bf2-474f-aed8-172a9d722276"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The sentence makes and supports a claim about Iberian orca behavior, claiming that the orcas&rsquo; destructive behavior may be the result of understimulation and citing as support the fact that as orcas have spent less time hunting (a stimulating activity), encounters with vessels have increased.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. While the sentence makes a claim about an orca deterrent, it misrepresents information from the notes. The workshop advised avoiding orcas pending further testing of TAST&rsquo;s efficacy; it didn&rsquo;t claim that TAST was effective. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence describes a workshop that addressed the orcas&rsquo; behavior; it doesn&rsquo;t make or support a claim about their behavior. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence does make a claim about orcas&rsquo; hunting behavior, it misrepresents information from the notes; the notes state that increased tuna populations have reduced orcas&rsquo; hunting time by 99%, not that tuna populations have increased by 99% because of the orcas&rsquo; behavior.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771170,"updateDate":1776362771170}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771170, 1776362771170),
    ($SATQ$79c6a01e$SATQ$, $SATQ$2a3f9924-5a73-48b0-b6b8-de51ecdaa9f5$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>The engineer Robert Fulton designed the <em>Clermont</em> steamboat in 1807.</li>
<li>He designed&nbsp;it in New York City.</li>
<li><em>Clermont</em> was the world&rsquo;s first commercially successful steamboat.</li>
<li>The city of Fulton, Missouri, is named after Robert Fulton.</li>
<li>New York City&rsquo;s Fulton Street is named after him.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to indicate how Fulton, Missouri, got its name. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"3ffbd1ed-4cf7-460e-b717-afef40040aed","content":"<p>Fulton, Missouri, shares its name with Fulton Street in New York City.</p>"},{"id":"f5e4903f-c1b1-44b3-8c6e-bd5328bcacf7","content":"<p>Fulton Street is in New York City, where the steamboat <em>Clermont</em> was designed in 1807.</p>"},{"id":"ee328138-0f52-4bf2-ba19-f76288390576","content":"<p>Designed in 1807 in New York City, <em>Clermont</em> was the first commercially successful steamboat.</p>"},{"id":"1f6befd5-9769-445a-82fc-8a9d723fa097","content":"<p>Fulton, Missouri, is named after Robert Fulton, designer of the first commercially successful steamboat.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["1f6befd5-9769-445a-82fc-8a9d723fa097"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence indicates how Fulton, Missouri, got its name, noting that the city was named after the designer of the first commercially successful steamboat, Robert Fulton.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence indicates that Fulton, Missouri, has the same name as a street in New York City; it doesn&rsquo;t indicate how the city of Fulton got its name. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence indicates that Fulton Street is in New York City; it doesn&rsquo;t indicate how Fulton, Missouri, got its name. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence provides details about Fulton&rsquo;s steamboat; it doesn&rsquo;t indicate how Fulton, Missouri, got its name.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"79c6a01e","external_id":"2a3f9924-5a73-48b0-b6b8-de51ecdaa9f5","vaultid":null,"uId":"3f71f363-6633-4d93-b852-a1c830263692","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The engineer Robert Fulton designed the <em>Clermont</em> steamboat in 1807.</li>\n<li>He designed&nbsp;it in New York City.</li>\n<li><em>Clermont</em> was the world&rsquo;s first commercially successful steamboat.</li>\n<li>The city of Fulton, Missouri, is named after Robert Fulton.</li>\n<li>New York City&rsquo;s Fulton Street is named after him.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to indicate how Fulton, Missouri, got its name. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"3ffbd1ed-4cf7-460e-b717-afef40040aed","content":"<p>Fulton, Missouri, shares its name with Fulton Street in New York City.</p>"},{"id":"f5e4903f-c1b1-44b3-8c6e-bd5328bcacf7","content":"<p>Fulton Street is in New York City, where the steamboat <em>Clermont</em> was designed in 1807.</p>"},{"id":"ee328138-0f52-4bf2-ba19-f76288390576","content":"<p>Designed in 1807 in New York City, <em>Clermont</em> was the first commercially successful steamboat.</p>"},{"id":"1f6befd5-9769-445a-82fc-8a9d723fa097","content":"<p>Fulton, Missouri, is named after Robert Fulton, designer of the first commercially successful steamboat.</p>"}],"keys":["1f6befd5-9769-445a-82fc-8a9d723fa097"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence indicates how Fulton, Missouri, got its name, noting that the city was named after the designer of the first commercially successful steamboat, Robert Fulton.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence indicates that Fulton, Missouri, has the same name as a street in New York City; it doesn&rsquo;t indicate how the city of Fulton got its name. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence indicates that Fulton Street is in New York City; it doesn&rsquo;t indicate how Fulton, Missouri, got its name. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence provides details about Fulton&rsquo;s steamboat; it doesn&rsquo;t indicate how Fulton, Missouri, got its name.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669204,"updateDate":1743430669204}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669204, 1743430669204),
    ($SATQ$7aac173e$SATQ$, $SATQ$291beae6-e79e-4376-80a4-d55d21f16b6a$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$702e8d72-ed3b-4338-b969-6db5427f6c9f$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Architect Julian Abele studied Gregorian and neo-Gothic architecture in Europe.</li>
<li>Abele worked for an architecture firm that was hired in 1924 to design buildings for Duke University&rsquo;s new campus.</li>
<li>Most of the buildings on Duke&rsquo;s campus were designed in the Gregorian or neo-Gothic architectural styles.</li>
<li>At the time, Abele was not formally credited with designing the buildings.</li>
<li>Based on the buildings&rsquo; architectural styles, historians believe Abele designed most of the campus buildings.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to specify why historians believe Abele designed most of Duke&rsquo;s campus buildings. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"dcc33d37-aee0-4e99-a94c-6680fc324773","content":"<p>Given that most of the buildings on Duke&rsquo;s campus feature architectural styles that Abele had studied in Europe, historians believe Abele is the one who designed them.</p>"},{"id":"3309b7e0-6d43-478c-a75c-579b8b405152","content":"<p>Though Abele wasn&rsquo;t formally credited at the time, historians believe he designed most of the buildings on Duke&rsquo;s campus.</p>"},{"id":"f4b86505-abaf-4b0f-97c2-a75bf029e0f3","content":"<p>Most of Duke&rsquo;s campus buildings, which were designed by a firm Abele worked for, were designed in the Gregorian and neo-Gothic architectural styles.</p>"},{"id":"461c31b5-07e5-4d02-8459-15766e10e886","content":"<p>Abele, an architect who studied Gregorian and neo-Gothic architecture in Europe, is believed to have designed most of the buildings on Duke&rsquo;s campus.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["dcc33d37-aee0-4e99-a94c-6680fc324773"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The sentence specifies why historians believe Abele designed most of Duke&rsquo;s campus buildings, noting that most of the buildings feature architectural styles that Abele had studied. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. While the sentence explains that historians believe Abele designed most of Duke&rsquo;s campus buildings, it doesn&rsquo;t specify why historians hold that belief. Choice C is incorrect because the sentence emphasizes the architectural styles of Duke&rsquo;s campus buildings; it doesn&rsquo;t specify why historians believe Abele designed the buildings. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence explains that Abele is believed to have designed most of the buildings on Duke&rsquo;s campus, it doesn&rsquo;t specify why historians believe that he designed the buildings. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-007-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$873a29e7-2390-419a-b6de-82e18ca2e9c6$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"7aac173e","external_id":"291beae6-e79e-4376-80a4-d55d21f16b6a","vaultid":"702e8d72-ed3b-4338-b969-6db5427f6c9f","uId":"8f51fd67-051d-4b57-ad91-3728736e4b1c","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Architect Julian Abele studied Gregorian and neo-Gothic architecture in Europe.</li>\n<li>Abele worked for an architecture firm that was hired in 1924 to design buildings for Duke University&rsquo;s new campus.</li>\n<li>Most of the buildings on Duke&rsquo;s campus were designed in the Gregorian or neo-Gothic architectural styles.</li>\n<li>At the time, Abele was not formally credited with designing the buildings.</li>\n<li>Based on the buildings&rsquo; architectural styles, historians believe Abele designed most of the campus buildings.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to specify why historians believe Abele designed most of Duke&rsquo;s campus buildings. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"dcc33d37-aee0-4e99-a94c-6680fc324773","content":"<p>Given that most of the buildings on Duke&rsquo;s campus feature architectural styles that Abele had studied in Europe, historians believe Abele is the one who designed them.</p>"},{"id":"3309b7e0-6d43-478c-a75c-579b8b405152","content":"<p>Though Abele wasn&rsquo;t formally credited at the time, historians believe he designed most of the buildings on Duke&rsquo;s campus.</p>"},{"id":"f4b86505-abaf-4b0f-97c2-a75bf029e0f3","content":"<p>Most of Duke&rsquo;s campus buildings, which were designed by a firm Abele worked for, were designed in the Gregorian and neo-Gothic architectural styles.</p>"},{"id":"461c31b5-07e5-4d02-8459-15766e10e886","content":"<p>Abele, an architect who studied Gregorian and neo-Gothic architecture in Europe, is believed to have designed most of the buildings on Duke&rsquo;s campus.</p>"}],"keys":["dcc33d37-aee0-4e99-a94c-6680fc324773"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The sentence specifies why historians believe Abele designed most of Duke&rsquo;s campus buildings, noting that most of the buildings feature architectural styles that Abele had studied. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. While the sentence explains that historians believe Abele designed most of Duke&rsquo;s campus buildings, it doesn&rsquo;t specify why historians hold that belief. Choice C is incorrect because the sentence emphasizes the architectural styles of Duke&rsquo;s campus buildings; it doesn&rsquo;t specify why historians believe Abele designed the buildings. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence explains that Abele is believed to have designed most of the buildings on Duke&rsquo;s campus, it doesn&rsquo;t specify why historians believe that he designed the buildings. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-007-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"873a29e7-2390-419a-b6de-82e18ca2e9c6","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959313,"updateDate":1691007959313}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959313, 1691007959313),
    ($SATQ$7c3f0145$SATQ$, $SATQ$adae1e30-0161-4e31-b988-b0581ead48a4$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In a 2022 analysis of 200 terms, researchers found a broad pattern of valence-dependent mutation for which negative words saw a faster rate of cognate replacement&mdash;<span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> the rate at which a word will be replaced over time with a noncognate form. Adjectives (e.g., &ldquo;afraid&rdquo;) saw the largest effect; nouns (e.g., &ldquo;attack&rdquo;), meanwhile, saw the smallest.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"0e26ed7e-8826-4bba-811a-770f2174dda7","content":"<p>for example,</p>"},{"id":"7863462d-3b81-4453-a216-fbd6866cc2a9","content":"<p>likewise,</p>"},{"id":"0f9092b3-faf7-4e75-875d-36c0b335d1ef","content":"<p>in addition,</p>"},{"id":"2659edb8-a8a1-4e4a-be8f-d57ba7b5ac1c","content":"<p>that is,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["2659edb8-a8a1-4e4a-be8f-d57ba7b5ac1c"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. "That is" logically signals that the information that follows in the sentence clarifies a term used in the previous discussion of the researchers&rsquo; findings: cognate replacement.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because "for example" illogically signals that the information that follows in the sentence provides a specific example of the research findings described earlier. Instead, it clarifies a term introduced earlier in the sentence. Choice B is incorrect because "likewise" illogically signals that the information that follows in the sentence is similar to the previous information about the researchers&rsquo; findings. Instead, it clarifies a term introduced earlier in the sentence. Choice C is incorrect because "in addition" illogically signals that the information that follows in the sentence merely adds to the previous information about the researchers&rsquo; findings. Instead, it clarifies a term introduced earlier in the sentence.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"7c3f0145","external_id":"adae1e30-0161-4e31-b988-b0581ead48a4","vaultid":null,"uId":"c368d3bf-8d6b-4da6-b651-0782aee3c579","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In a 2022 analysis of 200 terms, researchers found a broad pattern of valence-dependent mutation for which negative words saw a faster rate of cognate replacement&mdash;<span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> the rate at which a word will be replaced over time with a noncognate form. Adjectives (e.g., &ldquo;afraid&rdquo;) saw the largest effect; nouns (e.g., &ldquo;attack&rdquo;), meanwhile, saw the smallest.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"0e26ed7e-8826-4bba-811a-770f2174dda7","content":"<p>for example,</p>"},{"id":"7863462d-3b81-4453-a216-fbd6866cc2a9","content":"<p>likewise,</p>"},{"id":"0f9092b3-faf7-4e75-875d-36c0b335d1ef","content":"<p>in addition,</p>"},{"id":"2659edb8-a8a1-4e4a-be8f-d57ba7b5ac1c","content":"<p>that is,</p>"}],"keys":["2659edb8-a8a1-4e4a-be8f-d57ba7b5ac1c"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. \"That is\" logically signals that the information that follows in the sentence clarifies a term used in the previous discussion of the researchers&rsquo; findings: cognate replacement.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because \"for example\" illogically signals that the information that follows in the sentence provides a specific example of the research findings described earlier. Instead, it clarifies a term introduced earlier in the sentence. Choice B is incorrect because \"likewise\" illogically signals that the information that follows in the sentence is similar to the previous information about the researchers&rsquo; findings. Instead, it clarifies a term introduced earlier in the sentence. Choice C is incorrect because \"in addition\" illogically signals that the information that follows in the sentence merely adds to the previous information about the researchers&rsquo; findings. Instead, it clarifies a term introduced earlier in the sentence.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061191,"updateDate":1755117061191}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061191, 1755117061191),
    ($SATQ$7c9d0e38$SATQ$, $SATQ$1ee0a4e4-27c2-4283-a0b1-5a9a1d15096b$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$bd2ae01d-c0dc-4814-9ef0-657b2235d5a9$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Roughly 96% of Australia&rsquo;s estimated 200,000 animal species are invertebrates.</li>
<li>Invertebrates of the order Hymenoptera, which consists of sawflies, wasps, bees, and ants, are estimated to total 14,800 species in Australia.</li>
<li>Invertebrates of the order Coleoptera, which consists of beetles and weevils, are estimated to total 28,200 species in Australia.</li>
<li>Some of these invertebrates&rsquo; populations are threatened by invasive bird and fish species.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to emphasize the different orders in which Australia&rsquo;s invertebrate animals are classified. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"3e68edc6-fed7-4111-a55f-36cef114783d","content":"<p>In Australia, 28,200 species are estimated to be beetles and weevils, both classified as invertebrates of the order Coleoptera.</p>"},{"id":"e7742ecc-1bf3-4f80-bba6-896c0adac2a4","content":"<p>Among Australia&rsquo;s many invertebrates, sawflies, wasps, bees, and ants belong to the order Hymenoptera, while beetles and weevils belong to the order Coleoptera.</p>"},{"id":"c3ae9813-f884-4b6d-bb51-fee0f6ea2db9","content":"<p>Many sawflies, wasps, bees, and ants of the order Hymenoptera are threatened by some of Australia&rsquo;s invasive bird and fish species.</p>"},{"id":"701a95e2-e81d-407e-85a3-bb36fc6731e9","content":"<p>The order Hymenoptera is estimated to make up 14,800 of Australia&rsquo;s 200,000 animal species.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["e7742ecc-1bf3-4f80-bba6-896c0adac2a4"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The sentence emphasizes the different orders that Australia&rsquo;s invertebrates belong to, specifying that sawflies, wasps, bees, and ants belong to the order Hymenoptera, whereas beetles and weevils belong to the order Coleoptera. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence only mentions one order, Coleoptera; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the different orders that Australia&rsquo;s invertebrates belong to. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence only mentions one order, Hymenoptera; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the different orders that Australia&rsquo;s invertebrates belong to. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence only mentions one order, Hymenoptera; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the different orders that Australia&rsquo;s invertebrates belong to. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-716-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$e7b718ac-dc45-43d6-a2f5-d255750c1e8f$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"7c9d0e38","external_id":"1ee0a4e4-27c2-4283-a0b1-5a9a1d15096b","vaultid":"bd2ae01d-c0dc-4814-9ef0-657b2235d5a9","uId":"71645d0c-f18a-4be0-b0db-e5587f6cd263","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Roughly 96% of Australia&rsquo;s estimated 200,000 animal species are invertebrates.</li>\n<li>Invertebrates of the order Hymenoptera, which consists of sawflies, wasps, bees, and ants, are estimated to total 14,800 species in Australia.</li>\n<li>Invertebrates of the order Coleoptera, which consists of beetles and weevils, are estimated to total 28,200 species in Australia.</li>\n<li>Some of these invertebrates&rsquo; populations are threatened by invasive bird and fish species.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to emphasize the different orders in which Australia&rsquo;s invertebrate animals are classified. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"3e68edc6-fed7-4111-a55f-36cef114783d","content":"<p>In Australia, 28,200 species are estimated to be beetles and weevils, both classified as invertebrates of the order Coleoptera.</p>"},{"id":"e7742ecc-1bf3-4f80-bba6-896c0adac2a4","content":"<p>Among Australia&rsquo;s many invertebrates, sawflies, wasps, bees, and ants belong to the order Hymenoptera, while beetles and weevils belong to the order Coleoptera.</p>"},{"id":"c3ae9813-f884-4b6d-bb51-fee0f6ea2db9","content":"<p>Many sawflies, wasps, bees, and ants of the order Hymenoptera are threatened by some of Australia&rsquo;s invasive bird and fish species.</p>"},{"id":"701a95e2-e81d-407e-85a3-bb36fc6731e9","content":"<p>The order Hymenoptera is estimated to make up 14,800 of Australia&rsquo;s 200,000 animal species.</p>"}],"keys":["e7742ecc-1bf3-4f80-bba6-896c0adac2a4"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The sentence emphasizes the different orders that Australia&rsquo;s invertebrates belong to, specifying that sawflies, wasps, bees, and ants belong to the order Hymenoptera, whereas beetles and weevils belong to the order Coleoptera. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence only mentions one order, Coleoptera; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the different orders that Australia&rsquo;s invertebrates belong to. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence only mentions one order, Hymenoptera; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the different orders that Australia&rsquo;s invertebrates belong to. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence only mentions one order, Hymenoptera; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the different orders that Australia&rsquo;s invertebrates belong to. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-716-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"e7b718ac-dc45-43d6-a2f5-d255750c1e8f","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959348,"updateDate":1691007959348}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959348, 1691007959348)
